{"name":"Amber","hex":"#ffbf00","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Amber","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A female given name from English, popular in the 1980s and the 1990s.","A surname of uncertain origin."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A male given name from Hindi.","A city in Rajasthan, India, also known as Amer."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Amber"}],"entries":[{"title":"Amber","blocks":[{"text":"Amber is fossilized tree resin. It has been appreciated for its color and natural beauty since Neolithic times, and worked as a gemstone since classical antiquity. Amber is used in jewelry and as a healing agent in folk medicine."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Amber2.jpg/330px-Amber2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amber","wikidata":"Q25381"},{"title":"Amber (color)","blocks":[{"text":"The color amber is a pure chroma color, located on the color wheel midway between the colors of yellow and orange. The color name is derived from the material also known as amber, which is commonly found in a range of yellow-orange-brown-red colors; likewise, as a color, amber can refer to a range of yellow-orange colors. In English, the first recorded use of the term as a color name, rather than a reference to the specific substance, was in 1500."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Ant_in_amber_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Ant_in_amber_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amber_(color)","wikidata":"Q679355"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Amber_Bernstein_many_stones.jpg/500px-Amber_Bernstein_many_stones.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Amber_Bernstein_many_stones.jpg/500px-Amber_Bernstein_many_stones.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Unpolished amber stones","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAmber_Bernstein_many_stones.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Amber_Fisher.jpg/500px-Amber_Fisher.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Amber_Fisher.jpg/500px-Amber_Fisher.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Fishing for amber on the coast of Baltic Sea, near Gdańsk, Poland. Winter storms throw out amber nuggets.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAmber_Fisher.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Gouttes-drops-resine-2.jpg/500px-Gouttes-drops-resine-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Gouttes-drops-resine-2.jpg/500px-Gouttes-drops-resine-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Wood resin, the source of amber","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGouttes-drops-resine-2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Baltic-amber-fossils-inclusions.jpg/500px-Baltic-amber-fossils-inclusions.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Baltic-amber-fossils-inclusions.jpg/1280px-Baltic-amber-fossils-inclusions.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Baltic-amber-fossils-inclusions.jpg/500px-Baltic-amber-fossils-inclusions.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Baltic amber with inclusions","link":"https://en.